**What to expect: counties.** A county enters when it has a Black cell of at least 50 tested students in every one of the 3 grades; a county with no kept cell in some grade fails the rule. Counties with any kept Black cell and counties meeting the rule, by state:

~~~~
(3 vars, 6 obs)






  +----------------------------------------------------------------+
  |           fips   counties_with_black_cells   counties_eligible |
  |----------------------------------------------------------------|
  |        Alabama                          48                  45 |
  |        Georgia                          97                  85 |
  |      Louisiana                          58                  57 |
  |    Mississippi                          68                  61 |
  | North Carolina                          65                  63 |
  |----------------------------------------------------------------|
  | South Carolina                          44                  44 |
  +----------------------------------------------------------------+

~~~~

The proficiency file has 3,031 county-grade-subgroup cells in 355 counties; 293 counties have all three subgroups in all three grades. The White cell is the one missing in every incomplete cell, in majority-Black counties where fewer than 50 White students were tested in any district.

**What to expect: covariates.** One row per county with the child poverty rate, the Black share of enrollment, the between-district dispersion of the Black share and the log number of districts. The number of counties whose covariates had to be imputed with the sample mean is written to `output/tables/covariate_imputation.csv`; in this run:

~~~~
(3 vars, 4 obs)


  +--------------------------------------------------------------+
  |               variable   counties_imputed   sample_mean_used |
  |--------------------------------------------------------------|
  |           poverty_rate                  0           .2752136 |
  |            share_black                  0           .4520424 |
  |           seg_bw_index                  0           .0824729 |
  | district_fragmentation                  0           .5309059 |
  +--------------------------------------------------------------+



    Variable |        Obs        Mean    Std. dev.       Min        Max
-------------+---------------------------------------------------------
poverty_rate |        355    .2752136    .0981978     .04724    .653871
 share_black |        355    .4520424    .2374937   .0408341   .9957717
seg_bw_index |        355    .0824729    .1146685          0   .4165392
district_f~n |        355    .5309059    .6834667          0   4.356709
 n_districts |        355    2.476056    5.005236          1         78

~~~~

**Decisions the code takes, and why.** Every merge uses the numeric value of the district id, because the portal zero-pads the seven-digit `leaid` in some files and not in others; a string merge loses every district in states whose FIPS code starts with zero. Directory records whose county code lies in another state are dropped (one record in this run). The county proficiency rate is the mean of district rates weighted by the number tested; the number tested is the plain sum (`rawsum`), never the weighted sum. The composition and dispersion measures use the districts with a Black share in the enrollment file, and the district count every district. The four ordered categories are reconstructed from the single proficiency cut under the assumption of a normal distribution with unit variance and cut scores at -1, 0 and 1; a rate of exactly 0 or 100 would be bounded at 0.5 and 99.5 percent (none occurs in this scope); see the README for what this implies.

**If it fails.** `variable race not found` or a race label other than `Total`, `White` and `Black` means the portal changed its value labels; check `label list` on the raw file. `merge` reporting zero matches between SAIPE and the directory means the district id arrived in a new format; compare `leaid` in the two raw files.

## 4. HETOP estimation (code/03_estimate_gaps.do, parts 1 and 2)

**What it does.** Fits a heteroskedastic ordered probit model (Reardon, Shear, Castellano and Ho 2017) to every county-grade cell with three groups (all students, White, Black) and four categories, with `hetop` (`numcats(4) identify(sums) gaps minsize(20)`, the same call for every cell), and saves both the identification-metric and the standardized estimates; the gaps use the standardized ones.

**The command.** `do code/03_estimate_gaps.do` through `run_all.do` (`RUN_STEPS` `03`). Parts 3 to 6 of the same script are described in step 5.

**What to expect.** 1,065 cells fit in   58.7 seconds, about  0.06 seconds per cell. Convergence by state:

<table class="dt">
<thead><tr><th>State</th><th>County-grade cells</th><th>Converged</th><th>Incomplete (a subgroup missing)</th><th>Failed or not converged</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Alabama</td><td>135</td><td>105</td><td>30</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Georgia</td><td>255</td><td>230</td><td>25</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Louisiana</td><td>171</td><td>155</td><td>16</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mississippi</td><td>183</td><td>124</td><td>59</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>North Carolina</td><td>189</td><td>174</td><td>15</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>South Carolina</td><td>132</td><td>113</td><td>19</td><td>0</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

Incomplete cells are those where one subgroup is missing after the size rule; they are written out with missing estimates and do not enter the gap computation. Because the categories were reconstructed from one proficiency rate, the standardized SDs sit close to 1:

~~~~


                HETOP SD, standardized metric
-------------------------------------------------------------
      Percentiles      Smallest
 1%     .9075923       .8050529
 5%     .9336957       .8050869
10%      .945683       .8190114       Obs               2,703
25%     .9619023       .8219321       Sum of wgt.       2,703

50%     .9745366                      Mean           .9729528
                        Largest       Std. dev.      .0234185
75%     .9879546        1.05285
90%     .9975298       1.061177       Variance       .0005484
95%     1.002059       1.152888       Skewness      -.6608888
99%     1.020701        1.18785       Kurtosis       11.44072

~~~~

**If it fails.** `hetop` not found: `ssc install hetop`. A cell that fails to converge, or that returns fewer than three groups, is counted and skipped; the failure does not stop the run. Many failures at once usually mean a category column with zero counts everywhere, which happens with very small `MIN_CELL_N`.

## 5. Gaps, gapci, pooling and the regression ladder (code/03_estimate_gaps.do, parts 3 to 6)

**What it does.** Computes the White-Black gap V (Ho 2009) and its delta-method standard error for every cell with the Mata routine `compute_gaps_vec()`; compares V with the direct difference of probits and its binomial standard error; pools the grade-level gaps of each county into one estimate with a precision-weighted random-intercept model (Hedges and Vevea 1998); and estimates the four-model ladder.

**The command.** Same script as step 4.

**What to expect: the companion command.** `gapci` reproduces `compute_gaps_vec()` cell by cell; the script stops if the two disagree beyond numerical precision. Its output for one cell:

~~~~



------------------------------------------------------------
 Standardized achievement gap (HETOP / V-statistic)
------------------------------------------------------------
  Group 1 (reference): mean =   0.183  SD =   0.982
  Group 2            : mean =  -0.422  SD =   0.986
  ------------------------------------------------------
  V statistic      =   0.615
  SE (delta method)=   0.080
  95% CI          = [  0.459,   0.771]
------------------------------------------------------------


~~~~

**What to expect: HETOP against the direct estimator.** Across   901 cells the correlation between V and the direct probit difference is 0.9987, with a median absolute difference of 0.0054. The binomial standard error is 1.30 times the HETOP standard error at the median (10th to 90th percentile 1.22 to 1.35). The HETOP standard errors treat the reconstructed categories as observed; the binomial ones use only the information the published rate carries. Both standard errors are kept in `gaps_county_level.dta`, and the county table reports both 95% intervals.

**What to expect: pooling.**   901 cell gaps pooled into 307 county estimates. The model multiplies every term, the county intercept included, by the square root of the cell weight before `mixed` fits it; on the transformed scale the county effect is a random coefficient on that square root. Fixed part: intercept  0.794, grade slope -0.0040 (SE 0.0027); county variance 0.0343; pooled gap at the reference grade 6: 0.770. The residual variance of the weighted model is  3.37; it would be 1 if the cell standard errors captured all the variation of a county's gap across grades. The pooled standard error is computed from the sum of cell precisions, so it is too small by a factor of about the square root of that variance. The median county gap is 0.755, the 10th and 90th percentiles 0.580 and 0.952.

~~~~


     State FIPS code |      Freq.     Percent        Cum.
---------------------+-----------------------------------
             Alabama |         36       11.73       11.73
             Georgia |         79       25.73       37.46
           Louisiana |         52       16.94       54.40
         Mississippi |         44       14.33       68.73
      North Carolina |         58       18.89       87.62
      South Carolina |         38       12.38      100.00
---------------------+-----------------------------------
               Total |        307      100.00


    Variable |        Obs        Mean    Std. dev.       Min        Max
-------------+---------------------------------------------------------
gap_wb_poo~d |        307    .7696789    .1579756   .4379011   1.472388
gap_wb_poo~e |        307    .0620722    .0281333   .0135686   .1784398
gap_wb_poo~t |        307    .0801074    .0364108   .0172893   .2389078
    n_grades |        307    2.934853    .3167289          1          3

~~~~

