<tr><td>White-Black gap</td><td>All states</td><td>888</td><td>0.796</td><td>0.807</td></tr>
<tr><td>White-Black gap</td><td>Alabama</td><td>105</td><td>0.769</td><td>0.735</td></tr>
<tr><td>White-Black gap</td><td>Georgia</td><td>227</td><td>0.868</td><td>0.849</td></tr>
<tr><td>White-Black gap</td><td>Louisiana</td><td>149</td><td>0.737</td><td>0.802</td></tr>
<tr><td>White-Black gap</td><td>Mississippi</td><td>120</td><td>0.824</td><td>0.833</td></tr>
<tr><td>White-Black gap</td><td>North Carolina</td><td>174</td><td>0.750</td><td>0.773</td></tr>
<tr><td>White-Black gap</td><td>South Carolina</td><td>113</td><td>0.796</td><td>0.778</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

Across all states the county means for all students correlate at 0.971 (Pearson) and 0.979 (Spearman) over 1,056 county-grade cells; the White-Black gaps at 0.796 and 0.807 over   888 cells. The pooled county gaps against SEDA's grade-averaged gaps correlate at 0.873 (Pearson) and 0.861 (Spearman) over 306 counties.

<figure class="chart card">
<img src="figures/figure4_seda_validation.png" alt="Scatter plots of pipeline estimates against SEDA estimates, county means and White-Black gaps, with fitted lines">
<figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Pipeline estimates against SEDA 6.0 estimates for the same counties, grades and school year, both standardized within state and grade. Left: county means for all students. Right: White-Black gaps. Colors identify states. Source: this run.</figcaption>
</figure>

**Why levels are not compared.** SEDA links its estimates to the NAEP scale and reports them in national student-level standard deviation units; this pipeline works from the single proficiency cut in the public files and standardizes within each state, grade and year. Within a state and grade the SEDA scales are affine transformations of the state-standardized mean, so the comparable quantities are the ordering and the linear association of counties, not the levels.

**If it fails.** `copy` failing on the Stanford Digital Repository URL means no connection or a changed file name; the current names are listed at https://edopportunity.org/opportunity/data/downloads/. SEDA 6.0 ends with spring 2019 (school year 2018-19) for every geographic unit; later school years are released separately as the Educational Opportunity Trends Project, with district and state estimates and no county files, so this step has no comparator for them and stops with a message. Set `RUN_STEPS` to `"01 02 03 05 docs"` in that case: step 05 then writes a placeholder in place of the SEDA table, skips Figure 4, and the documents show the validation numbers as missing.

## 7. Figures and tables (code/05_figures_tables.do)

**What it does.** Downloads the Census cartographic boundary file for counties (2018, 1:20,000,000), takes county names from it, draws the four figures and writes the HTML tables used by the brief and this manual.

**The command.** `do code/05_figures_tables.do` through `run_all.do` (`RUN_STEPS` `05`).

**What to expect.** In `output/figures`: `figure1_county_gaps`, `figure2_county_map` and `figure3_gap_poverty` in SVG and PNG, and `figure4_seda_validation` in PNG only (an SVG would carry the standardized SEDA values as coordinates); copies in `docs/figures`. In `output/tables`, written by this step: `county_gaps.csv` and `.html`, `state_summary`, `exclusions_summary`, `seda_correlations.html` and `convergence.html`; the esttab tables and the CSV summaries of steps 3 to 6 are written by those steps. The state summary:

