<header class="mast wrap">
<div class="kicker">A brief of the research · Six Southern states, school year 2018-19</div>
<h1>County achievement gaps recovered from proficiency counts</h1>
<p class="deck">Districts report how many students took the state test and what share met the standard, and every state sets its own test. This page recovers from that thin record the gap between White and Black students' average mathematics achievement in 307 counties, and checks the result against an independent archive.</p>
<div class="byline">By <b>Felipe Hernán Rodríguez Villalta</b>, 2026 · EDFacts, Common Core of Data and SAIPE through the Urban Institute Education Data Portal · Stata and Mata; validated against SEDA 6.0</div>
</header>

<main class="wrap">

<div class="prose">
<p><span class="lead">The federal record of state testing is public, complete and coarse. For every district, grade and student group, EDFacts publishes the number of students tested and the share who scored at or above the state's proficient standard, in ranges when the group is small. It publishes no scores, and each state tests with its own instrument against its own standard.</span> A difference of ten percentage points in proficiency means one thing in a state with a demanding standard and another in a state with a lenient one, and the same difference means different things at the top and at the bottom of the distribution (Ho 2008).</p>
<p>This page reports what can nonetheless be learned from that record about the gap between White and Black students in mathematics, for the counties of Alabama, Georgia, Louisiana, Mississippi, North Carolina and South Carolina in school year 2018-19. The gaps are expressed in standard deviation units of student achievement, a metric that does not depend on the state's test or standard, so they can be placed side by side across states. The page describes differences in measured achievement between groups of children who go to school in very different conditions. It does not attribute those differences to the children, and it does not identify their causes.</p>
</div>

<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><div class="v">307</div><div class="l">counties with an estimate</div><div class="d">of 355 with a district of at least 50 Black test takers in grades 4, 6 and 8</div></div>
<div class="tile"><div class="v">  448,869</div><div class="l">Black students tested</div><div class="d">and   510,121 White students in the 355 sample counties, three grades together</div></div>
<div class="tile"><div class="v">0.77</div><div class="l">standard deviations, pooled gap</div><div class="d">median county 0.76; 307 of 307 HETOP intervals above zero</div></div>
<div class="tile"><div class="v">0.97</div><div class="l">correlation with SEDA</div><div class="d">county means, 1,056 county-grade cells, within states</div></div>
</div>

<h2 class="chap" id="problem"><small>Chapter 1</small>Counts by category, not scores</h2>
<div class="prose">
<p>States report test results to the federal government as counts of students in ordered performance categories. The categories carry real information about where a group's scores lie, but the information is coarsened: a student at the bottom of "proficient" and one at the top are the same student in the count. Ho and Reardon (2012) showed how to estimate the gap between two groups from such counts, and Reardon, Shear, Castellano and Ho (2017) how to recover each group's mean and standard deviation. Their model, a heteroskedastic ordered probit, treats the categories as cuts through a continuous distribution and estimates where each group's distribution sits relative to cut scores shared by the groups in the fit, here the three groups of a county and grade.</p>
<p>The public files add a second coarsening. They publish one cut, the proficient standard, not the full set of categories. The pipeline behind this page therefore reconstructs four categories from each group's proficiency rate under two stated assumptions: scores within a group are normally distributed with the same spread in every group, and the state's cut scores sit at fixed positions around the proficient standard. The assumptions fix the spread; what the data identify is where each group sits relative to the standard, and so the distance between groups. The gap is summarized as Ho's (2009) V, which is unchanged by any increasing transformation of a state's scale and which, under the normality assumed here, equals the difference in means divided by the pooled standard deviation.</p>
<p>The gap is estimated for every county and grade, then the three grades of a county are pooled into one estimate with weights that favor the more precise grades, following the meta-analytic model of Hedges and Vevea (1998). District cells with fewer than 50 tested students are set aside before aggregation, because the published rates for small groups are too coarse; this rule excludes 5.4% of the Black students tested in the six states. Counties then enter only when at least 50 Black students were tested in each grade, which keeps 355 of the 518 counties and a further 0.5% of the remaining Black test takers outside the sample. In 48 of those counties no district had 50 White test takers in any grade, so a gap is estimated for 307 counties.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="chap" id="counties"><small>Chapter 2</small>What the counties show</h2>
<div class="prose">
<p>In every one of the 307 counties, White students' average exceeds Black students' average. The HETOP interval lies above zero in 307 counties and the wider binomial interval in 307. The pooled gap across counties is 0.77 standard deviations. The median county has a gap of 0.76, and the middle 80% of counties lie between 0.58 and 0.95. For scale, a gap of 0.8 standard deviations means that the median Black student scores near the 21st percentile of White students in the same county.</p>
<p>The four largest gaps are in Charleston (South Carolina) at 1.47, Fulton (Georgia) at 1.38, DeKalb (Georgia) at 1.36 and Jefferson (Alabama) at 1.26; the three smallest in Panola (Mississippi) at 0.44, Clayton (Georgia) at 0.45 and Long (Georgia) at 0.45. The largest gaps are in the largest metropolitan counties of their states: among the ten counties with the most test takers, 9 have gaps above the median county. The smallest gaps occur in counties of every size. The gap changes little across grades: the pooling model's slope on grade is -0.004 standard deviations per grade.</p>
</div>

