<h2 class="chap" id="context"><small>Chapter 3</small>Gaps and the conditions around them</h2>
<div class="prose">
<p>Racial achievement gaps are closely associated with racial differences in family resources, in the poverty of the schools students attend and in segregation (Reardon, Kalogrides and Shores 2019); income gaps show the same pattern (Reardon 2011). Two county characteristics available for every county are used here to place the gaps in context: the child poverty rate of the county, from the Census Bureau's small area estimates, and the structure of its school districts.</p>
<p>Across these counties the child poverty rate is at most weakly associated with the size of the gap. The slope of the gap on the poverty rate is -0.41 standard deviations per unit of the rate (robust standard error 0.23), distinguishable from zero only at the 10% level; across the observed range of poverty rates the fitted line in Figure 3 falls by about 0.20 standard deviations, and the rate accounts for   2% of the precision-weighted variation in county gaps. Counties with high child poverty rates have gaps as large or as small as counties with low rates. The structure of districts is more closely associated with the gap. Counties with more school districts have larger gaps: 0.10 standard deviations per log unit (standard error 0.02), or about 0.07 for a doubling of the number of districts, with poverty, the Black share of enrollment and the unevenness of that share across districts held constant. Together the four characteristics account for  25% of the variation in county gaps.</p>
<p>The count of districts admits more than one reading, and these data do not distinguish between them. In these states a county with several districts may be a county system beside one or more city systems, or, in North Carolina and Louisiana especially, a county whose charter schools are each counted as a district in the federal directory; the count therefore mixes institutional fragmentation with metropolitan size. Larger racial segregation between schools is associated with larger gaps (Reardon 2016; Reardon, Weathers, Fahle, Jang and Kalogrides 2024), but these data cannot say whether the larger gaps of metropolitan counties reflect more advantaged White students, more segregated schools, or both. The measure of how unevenly Black students are spread across a county's districts adds nothing once the number of districts is in the model, and it is zero by construction in the 144 single-district counties, where sorting happens between schools inside the district and is invisible in a district-level file.</p>
</div>

