<h2 class="chap" id="validation"><small>Chapter 4</small>How the estimates are checked</h2>
<div class="prose">
<p>The Stanford Education Data Archive (SEDA) estimates county means and gaps from the same federal collection, with the full set of performance categories, a different model specification and a link to the national NAEP scale. Its sixth release covers school year 2018-19 and provides an independent set of estimates against which this pipeline's can be judged. Levels cannot be compared: SEDA's scale is national, while this pipeline's gaps are in within-county standard deviation units and its validation means are standardized within each state and grade. Within a state and grade, however, SEDA's values are a linear transformation of the state-standardized ones, so the ordering of counties and the linear association can be compared, through correlations computed within states.</p>
<p>For county means of all students the correlation is 0.97 (Pearson) and 0.98 (Spearman) over 1,056 county-grade cells. For the White-Black gap, which is a difference of two noisier quantities, the correlation is 0.80 and 0.81 over   888 cells, and 0.87 for the pooled county gaps against SEDA's grade-averaged gaps over 306 counties. A second check is internal: the gap can be computed directly as the difference between the probits of the two groups' proficiency rates, with a standard error from the binomial variance of each rate. The two estimates agree to a correlation of 0.9987, but the binomial standard error is 1.30 times the HETOP standard error at the median, because the model's standard errors treat the reconstructed categories as observed. Both intervals are reported.</p>
</div>

