<h2 class="chap" id="supports"><small>Chapter 5</small>What the evidence supports, and what it does not</h2>
<div class="prose">
<ul>
<li>In school year 2018-19, the average mathematics score of White students exceeded that of Black students in every one of the 307 counties, by 0.44 to 1.47 standard deviations, with a pooled estimate of 0.77.</li>
<li>The size of the gap varies across counties far more than the uncertainty of any single estimate: the typical 95% interval is 0.23 standard deviations wide, while the spread between the 10th and 90th percentile county is 0.37.</li>
<li>Within these states the county poverty rate is at most weakly associated with the size of the gap; the number of school districts is more closely associated with it. Both associations are descriptive and admit more than one reading.</li>
<li>The estimates order counties the way SEDA does, with correlations near 0.97 for means and 0.80 for gaps.</li>
</ul>
<p>The sample is the counties with a district of at least 50 Black test takers in every grade, in six states where most districts are county systems (144 of the 307 counties have a single district). It excludes the 163 counties with no district of that size in some grade, and the 48 sample counties with no White cell of that size have no gap estimate; the pattern of association may differ in states with fragmented district systems. The estimates rest on one proficiency cut per group and on the stated assumptions about the shape of the score distribution; the HETOP intervals shown are narrower than the information in the published rates warrants, and the pooled intervals do not carry the variation of a county's gap across grades. Nothing on this page identifies a cause: the gaps are differences in measured achievement between groups of children schooled under different conditions, not differences in the children.</p>
</div>

<div class="pull">The published record is coarse. Read with an appropriate model and checked against an independent set of estimates, it places every county on one scale, and in each of the 307 counties the difference in measured mathematics achievement between White and Black students is large.</div>

<h2 class="chap" id="methods"><small>About this page</small>Data, methods and protection</h2>
<div class="methods">
<h3>Methods in brief</h3>
<dl>
<dt>Data</dt><dd>EDFacts mathematics assessment results by district, grade and race (students tested and the midpoint of the published proficiency range), the Common Core of Data district directory and enrollment by race, and the Census Bureau's Small Area Income and Poverty Estimates for school districts, all for school year 2018-19 and all retrieved from the Urban Institute Education Data Portal with the <code>educationdata</code> Stata package. The download took 14.8 minutes.</dd>
<dt>Sample</dt><dd>District-grade-subgroup cells with fewer than 50 tested students are excluded before aggregation (5.4% of Black test takers); cells suppressed by the Department of Education (601 rows, all small) are dropped and never reconstructed. A county enters when it has a Black cell of at least 50 tested students in every grade: 355 counties, of which 307 have a White cell of that size in at least one grade, and so a gap estimate, and 293 in all three grades.</dd>
<dt>Estimation</dt><dd>Heteroskedastic ordered probit (Reardon, Shear, Castellano and Ho 2017) with the <code>hetop</code> command, one fit per county and grade with three groups (all students, White, Black) and four categories reconstructed from the proficiency rate; 1,065 county-grade cells in  59 seconds. Gap V (Ho 2009) and its delta-method standard error for every cell in one Mata operation; the companion command <code>gapci</code> does the same for one pair of groups.</dd>
<dt>Pooling and models</dt><dd>Grade gaps pooled per county with a precision-weighted random-intercept model (Hedges and Vevea 1998), evaluated at grade 6. Four nested precision-weighted regressions of the pooled gap on child poverty, log number of districts, Black share of enrollment and between-district dispersion of that share, with robust standard errors.</dd>
<dt>Validation</dt><dd>Within-state HETOP refit across counties for each state, grade and group, correlated with SEDA 6.0 county means and White-Black gaps (cohort-standardized scale) after standardization within state and grade; and the direct probit-difference estimator with binomial standard errors as an internal check.</dd>
<dt>Protection</dt><dd>All sources are public and aggregated. Suppressed cells are never filled in or recovered by subtraction; no estimate on this page or in the tables rests on a cell with fewer than 50 tested students; subgroup estimates appear at the county level only; the raw downloads and the SEDA file are not redistributed, and the validation figure is published as an image without the underlying values. The repository's README and manual state these rules and account for every excluded cell.</dd>
<dt>Sources behind the figures</dt><dd>U.S. Department of Education, EDFacts; National Center for Education Statistics, Common Core of Data; U.S. Census Bureau, SAIPE; all via the Education Data Portal (Urban Institute), under the ODC Attribution License. Reardon, Ho, Shear, Fahle, saliba and Kalogrides (2025), Stanford Education Data Archive, version 6.0. Census Bureau cartographic boundary files, 2018.</dd>
</dl>
</div>

